1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,640
Ever feel like the Cabbage Man
from Avatar: The Last Airbender?

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:08,100
You watch your Commander plans crash
and burn, and you scream "MY CABBAGES!"

3
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:10,050
for the fifth time this week.

4
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:12,070
Yep. I've been there.

5
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:13,370
Every game.

6
00:00:13,410 --> 00:00:14,450
Another plan ruined.

7
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:15,890
Another cabbage gone.

8
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:21,260
But what if we stopped losing our
cabbages… and started weaponizing them?

9
00:00:21,375 --> 00:00:23,915
Hello everyone, this is Wil
D. Card with Wild Card Commander.

10
00:00:23,935 --> 00:00:28,115
And welcome to the Cabbage Converter: the
deck where we take ordinary cabbages and

11
00:00:28,135 --> 00:00:31,195
turn them into robots, bears,
wolves, even dinosaurs.

12
00:00:31,635 --> 00:00:35,135
Basically, it's a machine that
turns cabbages into nuclear weapons.

13
00:00:35,435 --> 00:00:36,435
I'm not kidding.

14
00:00:36,455 --> 00:00:40,915
I once turned 73 cabbages into bears to
kill the table, and my opponent got up and

15
00:00:40,935 --> 00:00:43,015
walked over to a wall
for, like, five minutes.

16
00:00:43,805 --> 00:00:46,365
And the whole thing comes
in around two hundred bucks.

17
00:00:46,745 --> 00:00:50,905
About $214 when I checked, and thirty
of that is the Cabbage Man himself.

18
00:00:51,085 --> 00:00:53,985
I built it because I was
tired of playing normal Magic.

19
00:00:54,145 --> 00:00:58,245
I wanted to sit down, say "Okay, I tap
twenty cabbages," and watch everyone's

20
00:00:58,265 --> 00:00:59,165
faces.

21
00:00:59,185 --> 00:01:02,285
So if you want to make a stupid amount
of mana and then bury your friends in

22
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:04,085
vegetables, you've found your deck.

23
00:01:04,425 --> 00:01:05,785
I'm going to teach you four things.

24
00:01:05,805 --> 00:01:07,405
One, what hand to keep.

25
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:09,885
Two, what you should
be doing by turn five.

26
00:01:09,905 --> 00:01:13,285
Three, how to win, including
three secret win cons.

27
00:01:13,305 --> 00:01:17,025
And four, the cards that make the deck
sing, plus the upgrades if you want to

28
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:17,945
take it further.

29
00:01:17,965 --> 00:01:21,805
One of those secret win cons is so
stupid it really should never work.

30
00:01:21,825 --> 00:01:22,825
But it does.

31
00:01:23,425 --> 00:01:25,625
By the end, you won't just
be a cabbage merchant.

32
00:01:25,645 --> 00:01:30,225
You'll be a cabbage master, the person at
the table making everyone go, "Wait… how

33
00:01:30,245 --> 00:01:31,245
many cabbages?"

34
00:01:31,446 --> 00:01:33,426
Introducing the Cabbage Merchant.

35
00:01:33,926 --> 00:01:36,726
Three mana, mono-green,
straight out of the Avatar set.

36
00:01:37,146 --> 00:01:40,546
Whenever an opponent casts a
noncreature spell, we make a Food.

37
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:41,766
That's a cabbage.

38
00:01:41,926 --> 00:01:45,346
And we can tap two cabbages
for one mana of any color.

39
00:01:45,566 --> 00:01:49,666
Oh, and cabbages are Foods, so
they're artifacts and tokens.

40
00:01:50,186 --> 00:01:50,766
Remember that.

41
00:01:50,786 --> 00:01:52,026
It matters a lot later.

42
00:01:52,606 --> 00:01:54,686
He does have one little downside.

43
00:01:55,006 --> 00:01:58,846
Whenever a creature deals combat damage
to us, we have to sacrifice a Food.

44
00:01:59,166 --> 00:02:00,066
Don't worry about it.

45
00:02:00,086 --> 00:02:01,886
We've got it covered, and
I'll show you how later.

46
00:02:02,115 --> 00:02:07,515
The whole deck runs on one simple truth:
the Cabbage Man makes an absurd amount of

47
00:02:07,535 --> 00:02:10,155
cabbages. Way more than
you think is possible.

48
00:02:10,415 --> 00:02:14,535
I'm talking "the store ran out of
token cards" levels of cabbage madness.

49
00:02:14,855 --> 00:02:19,155
We've had to use dice, poker chips and
tokens borrowed from other tables just to

50
00:02:19,175 --> 00:02:20,175
keep track.

51
00:02:20,295 --> 00:02:21,375
So here's the plan.

52
00:02:21,615 --> 00:02:25,415
We help our opponents cast more spells
with group hug, we stack cabbages like

53
00:02:25,435 --> 00:02:29,555
it's harvest season, and then we use all
that Food and mana to pull off the dumbest

54
00:02:29,575 --> 00:02:31,115
plays you've ever seen.

55
00:02:31,515 --> 00:02:32,875
Let's start with the opening hand.

56
00:02:33,093 --> 00:02:35,693
Mulligans with this deck are
easy, and they're consistent.

57
00:02:35,913 --> 00:02:37,653
We only care about two things.

58
00:02:37,673 --> 00:02:42,393
First, and most importantly, we want the
Cabbage Merchant out as fast as possible.

59
00:02:42,493 --> 00:02:44,993
The sooner he's working,
the more cabbages we get.

60
00:02:45,173 --> 00:02:49,893
So look for a one-mana dork and two lands:
land and dork on turn one, then land and

61
00:02:49,913 --> 00:02:51,613
Cabbage Merchant on turn two.

62
00:02:51,873 --> 00:02:54,393
Don't be afraid to go down to
five or six cards to find that.

63
00:02:54,413 --> 00:02:56,833
I promise the Cabbage Man
will take care of you.

64
00:02:56,853 --> 00:03:00,303
I once kept a four-card hand:
Forest, Forest, Llanowar

65
00:03:00,613 --> 00:03:01,893
Elves and a Temple Bell.

66
00:03:01,913 --> 00:03:02,913
That's all I had.

67
00:03:03,173 --> 00:03:04,173
I still won.

68
00:03:05,193 --> 00:03:08,593
Second, we want some card
draw, some group hug, or both.

69
00:03:08,713 --> 00:03:11,893
And a lot of our cards do both,
like Howling Mine and Temple Bell.

70
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:13,547
Quick one before the good stuff.

71
00:03:13,707 --> 00:03:16,367
If you want more deck
breakdowns like this, subscribe.

72
00:03:16,567 --> 00:03:19,067
Because this is where it gets silly.

73
00:03:19,279 --> 00:03:21,639
So what should a good game
look like by turn five?

74
00:03:21,899 --> 00:03:25,199
On turn three, you want to be drawing
cards, and even something as simple as

75
00:03:25,219 --> 00:03:26,899
Harmonize is a great play.

76
00:03:27,019 --> 00:03:30,219
It's four mana, and with a
dork-into-Merchant start that's exactly

77
00:03:30,239 --> 00:03:31,599
what you've got on turn three.

78
00:03:31,619 --> 00:03:34,259
Plus whatever cabbages the
table has handed you by then.

79
00:03:34,679 --> 00:03:37,459
And this is where you become
everyone's best friend.

80
00:03:37,659 --> 00:03:40,699
Remember, the more our opponents
cast, the more we benefit.

81
00:03:40,879 --> 00:03:43,619
They say, "I need cards."
You give them cards.

82
00:03:43,799 --> 00:03:44,759
They want mana?

83
00:03:44,779 --> 00:03:45,779
You give them mana.

84
00:03:45,879 --> 00:03:49,419
And they keep casting spells,
and you keep getting cabbages.

85
00:03:49,799 --> 00:03:53,519
By turn five, you want the Cabbage Man
out, a pile of cabbages on your side, and

86
00:03:53,539 --> 00:03:56,079
the whole table casting
spells like it's their job.

87
00:03:56,199 --> 00:03:57,779
That's when things get chaotic.

88
00:03:58,079 --> 00:03:59,539
People are casting spells.

89
00:03:59,659 --> 00:04:02,179
People are casting spells to
stop other people's spells.

90
00:04:02,359 --> 00:04:05,639
And meanwhile, your cabbage production
is completely out of control.

91
00:04:05,899 --> 00:04:07,499
This is usually when someone asks,

92
00:04:07,819 --> 00:04:11,179
"Wait, how many Food tokens do you have?"
And you get to say something ridiculous

93
00:04:11,199 --> 00:04:12,199
like,

94
00:04:12,219 --> 00:04:13,839
"37. Wait, no, 41.

95
00:04:13,859 --> 00:04:15,499
You just cast two, and
they're still on the stack."

96
00:04:16,369 --> 00:04:19,269
Worried about having too much
mana and nothing to do with it?

97
00:04:19,609 --> 00:04:20,549
Not here.

98
00:04:20,569 --> 00:04:24,419
The deck is packed with mana sinks:
Retrofitter Foundry, Arch of Orazca,

99
00:04:24,439 --> 00:04:28,529
Wren's Run Packmaster, who pumps out
wolves, and of course Dark Depths.

100
00:04:28,649 --> 00:04:29,649
More on that one later.

101
00:04:29,709 --> 00:04:31,269
You'll never end up
sitting there turtling.

102
00:04:31,989 --> 00:04:35,289
From there, you're overflowing with Food
and mana, and the deck flips the switch

103
00:04:35,309 --> 00:04:38,649
from friendly hug dealer to
cabbage-fueled war engine.

104
00:04:38,909 --> 00:04:42,569
Your Food count stops being reasonable
and starts becoming "Hey judge, can we use

105
00:04:42,589 --> 00:04:43,589
dice instead?"

106
00:04:43,789 --> 00:04:47,289
True story: I once had to borrow tokens
from two separate neighboring pods.

107
00:04:47,389 --> 00:04:48,809
The judge started taking pictures.

108
00:04:49,125 --> 00:04:53,245
There are no infinite combos in this
list, and it's a bracket 2 deck: no Game

109
00:04:53,265 --> 00:04:55,505
Changers. It wouldn't be hard to add one.

110
00:04:55,525 --> 00:04:57,225
But infinite is boring.

111
00:04:57,625 --> 00:04:58,625
You know what's not boring?

112
00:04:58,805 --> 00:05:03,385
Attacking someone with a pile
of 7/7 dinosaur cabbages.

113
00:05:03,765 --> 00:05:04,765
That's art.

114
00:05:05,285 --> 00:05:09,725
So we focus on making a ridiculous amount
of stuff instead, and it's way more fun.

115
00:05:10,105 --> 00:05:12,445
The all-star is Displaced Dinosaurs.

116
00:05:12,885 --> 00:05:17,125
Once it's out, every artifact that enters
under our control comes in as a 7/7

117
00:05:17,145 --> 00:05:19,965
Dinosaur. Every cabbage
we make from then on?

118
00:05:19,985 --> 00:05:21,165
A 7/7 dino.

119
00:05:21,285 --> 00:05:22,585
And it's not just Food.

120
00:05:22,685 --> 00:05:25,385
We run a lot of cheap
artifacts, so your Sol Ring?

121
00:05:25,485 --> 00:05:26,905
Dino. Hot Dog Cart?

122
00:05:27,005 --> 00:05:29,085
Dino. That random Treasure token?

123
00:05:29,185 --> 00:05:31,145
Believe it or not, also a dino.

124
00:05:31,885 --> 00:05:35,645
If the dinosaurs don't stick around,
our next-best win con, and sometimes the

125
00:05:35,665 --> 00:05:38,555
better one, is Halsin, Emerald Archdruid.

126
00:05:38,885 --> 00:05:43,205
For one mana, Halsin turns any token
into a 4/4 Bear until end of turn.

127
00:05:43,485 --> 00:05:46,945
That might not sound like a lot, but to
kill a player from 40, you need ten mana

128
00:05:46,965 --> 00:05:48,185
and ten cabbages.

129
00:05:48,385 --> 00:05:49,785
For this deck, that's nothing.

130
00:05:50,125 --> 00:05:53,405
I've had turns where I made 30
bears and still had mana left over.

131
00:05:53,565 --> 00:05:56,925
The bears were just standing there
holding cabbages, and it was perfect.

132
00:05:57,345 --> 00:05:58,345
One rules thing.

133
00:05:58,485 --> 00:06:02,465
A bear can only attack if that cabbage has
been on your side since the start of your

134
00:06:02,485 --> 00:06:03,325
turn.

135
00:06:03,345 --> 00:06:04,425
Cabbages you made on their turns?

136
00:06:04,445 --> 00:06:05,305
Good to go.

137
00:06:05,325 --> 00:06:06,325
Same goes for the dinos.

138
00:06:06,785 --> 00:06:08,845
And if you don't draw
either of those, it's fine.

139
00:06:09,265 --> 00:06:13,185
Idol of Oblivion turns eight mana into
a 10/10, Retrofitter Foundry makes 4/4

140
00:06:13,205 --> 00:06:15,245
Constructs all game, and
there's more in the list.

141
00:06:15,265 --> 00:06:17,465
The full decklist is
linked in the description.

142
00:06:17,745 --> 00:06:19,985
Now, remember those three secret win cons?

143
00:06:20,425 --> 00:06:22,605
These are the ones that
make people go, "Wait.

144
00:06:22,785 --> 00:06:24,325
That's how you're attacking me?"

145
00:06:24,645 --> 00:06:26,625
First up is Dark Depths.

146
00:06:27,005 --> 00:06:31,005
It takes a while to build up, but once
you melt that last ice counter, your

147
00:06:31,025 --> 00:06:33,445
opponents' life totals are
the next thing melting.

148
00:06:33,865 --> 00:06:38,045
Nothing says Magic as Richard Garfield
intended quite like removing ice counters

149
00:06:38,065 --> 00:06:39,245
with cabbage money.

150
00:06:39,585 --> 00:06:43,305
I've had someone laugh so hard at a Dark
Depths paid for in cabbages that they

151
00:06:43,325 --> 00:06:44,745
conceded out of respect.

152
00:06:45,445 --> 00:06:46,905
Next is Night of the Sweets' Revenge.

153
00:06:47,285 --> 00:06:50,025
Most of the time it's a value piece,
and we'll get to that in a minute.

154
00:06:50,045 --> 00:06:51,685
But look at the bottom of the card.

155
00:06:51,885 --> 00:06:56,205
You pay seven mana, which is not a problem
for this deck, and sacrifice it, and every

156
00:06:56,225 --> 00:06:59,825
creature you control gets plus X plus
X, where X is the number of Foods you

157
00:06:59,845 --> 00:07:00,845
control.

158
00:07:00,865 --> 00:07:03,545
With the number of Foods we
make, that ends games fast.

159
00:07:03,725 --> 00:07:07,697
I once won with three 1/1
elves that became 52/52s.

160
00:07:08,185 --> 00:07:11,845
And the last one, the one that should
never work, is Karn, Living Legacy.

161
00:07:12,365 --> 00:07:15,645
Usually we just use his minus one to turn
spare mana into digging through our deck.

162
00:07:15,665 --> 00:07:20,225
But in those weird games where he lands
early and you keep ticking him up, his

163
00:07:20,245 --> 00:07:23,385
ultimate lets you tap any
artifact to deal one damage.

164
00:07:23,745 --> 00:07:26,145
Every cabbage becomes a
little nuclear weapon.

165
00:07:26,405 --> 00:07:27,985
This has happened exactly once.

166
00:07:28,205 --> 00:07:29,765
I had 47 cabbages.

167
00:07:29,945 --> 00:07:34,105
The player I was targeting looked at my
board, looked at me, and said, "I can't

168
00:07:34,125 --> 00:07:36,025
even be mad." And then he scooped.

169
00:07:36,110 --> 00:07:39,650
If you've made it this far, you're in for
a treat, because these are the cards that

170
00:07:39,670 --> 00:07:40,810
make the deck sing.

171
00:07:40,910 --> 00:07:41,770
Or scream.

172
00:07:41,790 --> 00:07:42,850
Depends on your perspective.

173
00:07:43,590 --> 00:07:44,830
Academy Manufactor.

174
00:07:45,070 --> 00:07:48,270
Whenever we'd make a Food, we get a
Food, a Treasure and a Clue instead.

175
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:50,590
With the Cabbage Man, that's all the time.

176
00:07:50,730 --> 00:07:52,610
This card is so good it should be illegal.

177
00:07:52,710 --> 00:07:53,810
It's not, I checked.

178
00:07:53,830 --> 00:07:54,830
But it should be.

179
00:07:55,230 --> 00:07:56,310
Clock of Omens.

180
00:07:56,530 --> 00:07:59,330
This one goes crazy with
even a handful of cabbages.

181
00:07:59,610 --> 00:08:01,510
Tap two cabbages to untap an artifact.

182
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:05,250
Untap Sol Ring, and every cabbage
is basically a colorless mana.

183
00:08:05,530 --> 00:08:09,690
Untap Temple Bell or Idol of Oblivion, and
that's two cabbages for a card, which is

184
00:08:09,710 --> 00:08:11,530
an absolutely absurd rate.

185
00:08:11,710 --> 00:08:15,250
I've drawn my entire deck with this, and
I've used it with Temple Bell to draw my

186
00:08:15,270 --> 00:08:16,050
opponents out of theirs.

187
00:08:16,070 --> 00:08:18,510
Sarinth Steelseeker.

188
00:08:18,890 --> 00:08:22,510
Every time an artifact enters, and with
the Cabbage Man that's constantly, we look

189
00:08:22,530 --> 00:08:23,890
at the top card of our library.

190
00:08:24,030 --> 00:08:25,850
If it's a land, it goes to our hand.

191
00:08:25,870 --> 00:08:27,470
If not, we can put it in the graveyard.

192
00:08:27,749 --> 00:08:30,329
These cards make every
cabbage count twice.

193
00:08:31,069 --> 00:08:35,789
Second Harvest makes a copy of every token
we control: every cabbage, every Treasure,

194
00:08:36,069 --> 00:08:37,069
everything.

195
00:08:37,369 --> 00:08:41,489
Night of the Sweets' Revenge and Jaheira,
Friend of the Forest let each cabbage tap

196
00:08:41,509 --> 00:08:42,849
for a mana on its own.

197
00:08:43,309 --> 00:08:47,329
Normally it takes two cabbages to make
one mana, so that doubles our output.

198
00:08:47,949 --> 00:08:50,249
Trust me, this gets out of hand fast.

199
00:08:50,989 --> 00:08:54,289
And Peregrin Took gives us an extra
Food every time we make tokens.

200
00:08:54,829 --> 00:08:59,669
Put just a couple of these next to the
Cabbage Man, say Jaheira and Peregrin, and

201
00:08:59,689 --> 00:09:04,229
every noncreature spell your opponents
cast gives you two cabbages, each tapping

202
00:09:04,249 --> 00:09:05,729
for a mana right away.

203
00:09:06,749 --> 00:09:07,929
Someone once asked me,

204
00:09:08,329 --> 00:09:13,209
"How much mana do you have?" And I just
pointed at the board and said, "Yes."

205
00:09:13,533 --> 00:09:15,453
Now, remember that downside?

206
00:09:15,933 --> 00:09:16,933
Here's the fix:

207
00:09:17,293 --> 00:09:18,293
Elephant Grass.

208
00:09:18,433 --> 00:09:19,433
It's one mana.

209
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:24,053
Black creatures can't attack you at all,
and everything else costs two extra mana

210
00:09:24,073 --> 00:09:25,593
per creature to swing at you.

211
00:09:25,933 --> 00:09:30,213
That cumulative upkeep goes up by one
every turn, but this deck makes so much

212
00:09:30,233 --> 00:09:31,693
mana you'll barely notice.

213
00:09:32,133 --> 00:09:35,473
If your friends start complaining,
just stop paying it and it goes away.

214
00:09:35,633 --> 00:09:38,193
Or keep paying, and keep making cabbages.

215
00:09:38,534 --> 00:09:39,184
All right.

216
00:09:39,204 --> 00:09:43,304
You've seen the cabbages, and you're ready
to go from farmers market to industrial

217
00:09:43,324 --> 00:09:44,324
agriculture.

218
00:09:44,744 --> 00:09:47,264
Here are the cards that
break the deck wide open.

219
00:09:48,004 --> 00:09:50,024
Unwinding Clock, about $21.

220
00:09:50,624 --> 00:09:53,084
Remember how all our
cabbages are artifacts?

221
00:09:53,504 --> 00:09:57,664
This untaps every one of them during
each other player's untap step.

222
00:09:57,884 --> 00:10:00,284
In a four-player game,
that's four times the mana.

223
00:10:00,744 --> 00:10:01,744
Absolutely disgusting.

224
00:10:02,504 --> 00:10:04,184
Fomori Vault, about $15.

225
00:10:04,924 --> 00:10:08,544
Discard a card to look at a number of
cards equal to the artifacts you control,

226
00:10:08,564 --> 00:10:09,564
and take one.

227
00:10:09,784 --> 00:10:12,864
With this many cabbages, you're basically
picking your favorite card out of your

228
00:10:12,884 --> 00:10:14,184
deck. Inventors'

229
00:10:14,684 --> 00:10:15,904
Fair, about $19.

230
00:10:16,524 --> 00:10:19,524
Free life every turn, since we'll
always have three artifacts.

231
00:10:19,804 --> 00:10:23,284
But the real prize is sacrificing
it to tutor up any artifact.

232
00:10:23,484 --> 00:10:25,664
It's basically a menu for war crimes.

233
00:10:26,384 --> 00:10:28,764
Both of those lands can just
come in for a couple of Forests.

234
00:10:29,844 --> 00:10:31,604
Collective Voyage, about $8.

235
00:10:32,004 --> 00:10:36,024
Great group hug: everyone chips in
mana, and everyone gets that many lands.

236
00:10:36,264 --> 00:10:38,904
I once convinced a table to pay 23 mana.

237
00:10:39,124 --> 00:10:42,164
And guess who had Halsin ready
to turn all that mana into bears?

238
00:10:42,684 --> 00:10:44,844
The Cabbage Man always wins the arms race.

239
00:10:45,844 --> 00:10:47,544
Horn of Greed, about $17.

240
00:10:48,204 --> 00:10:50,244
Everyone draws a card
when they play a land.

241
00:10:50,384 --> 00:10:54,144
It's perfect group hug, keeping our
opponents fueled up and ourselves too.

242
00:10:55,504 --> 00:10:56,504
Doubling Season,

243
00:10:56,704 --> 00:10:58,444
Parallel Lives and Primal Vigor.

244
00:10:58,584 --> 00:11:00,184
Doubling Season's about $32,

245
00:11:00,544 --> 00:11:03,824
Parallel Lives about $40,
and Primal Vigor about $15.

246
00:11:04,504 --> 00:11:08,764
Primal Vigor doubles everyone's tokens,
theirs included, which is very group hug

247
00:11:08,784 --> 00:11:09,784
of us.

248
00:11:09,944 --> 00:11:13,944
With the Cabbage Man, Academy Manufactor
and Peregrin Took out, a single

249
00:11:13,964 --> 00:11:17,244
noncreature spell from an opponent
can make you a dozen tokens.

250
00:11:17,624 --> 00:11:19,364
The judge will make you prove the math.

251
00:11:19,424 --> 00:11:20,364
You will.

252
00:11:20,384 --> 00:11:22,424
Nobody at the table
will be happy about it.

253
00:11:23,404 --> 00:11:27,544
All of these together are
about $166, so pick one or two.

254
00:11:27,864 --> 00:11:29,764
Unwinding Clock if you want raw power.

255
00:11:29,924 --> 00:11:31,744
Collective Voyage if
you like your friends.

256
00:11:31,964 --> 00:11:34,504
Doubling Season if you
hate math and love chaos.

257
00:11:35,064 --> 00:11:39,104
Even one of these turns the deck from fun
casual into "Why is the cabbage player the

258
00:11:39,124 --> 00:11:39,991
archenemy?"

259
00:11:40,011 --> 00:11:42,071
Look, I know this sounds ridiculous,

260
00:11:42,491 --> 00:11:43,531
because it is.

261
00:11:44,151 --> 00:11:48,071
Magic doesn't always have to be
about optimal lines and win rates.

262
00:11:48,571 --> 00:11:52,691
Sometimes it's about sitting down, pulling
out a pile of cards that makes everyone

263
00:11:52,711 --> 00:11:55,991
laugh, and then beating
them to death with produce.

264
00:11:56,691 --> 00:12:00,691
This deck has brought me more joy
than any tier-one strategy ever could.

265
00:12:01,051 --> 00:12:04,771
I've made friends with it, I've confused
judges with it, and I've won games I had

266
00:12:04,791 --> 00:12:06,031
no business winning.

267
00:12:06,551 --> 00:12:09,791
If you build it, tell me your highest
cabbage count in the comments.

268
00:12:10,091 --> 00:12:11,861
My record's somewhere past seventy.

269
00:12:11,991 --> 00:12:14,591
And tell me what you'd cut
to fit in Unwinding Clock.

270
00:12:15,271 --> 00:12:19,131
The next time you're sitting across from
someone's $2,000 optimized combo deck,

271
00:12:19,211 --> 00:12:22,911
just remember: you could be turning
cabbages into nuclear weapons instead.

272
00:12:23,691 --> 00:12:24,691
Thanks for watching,

273
00:12:24,911 --> 00:12:25,911
and remember:

274
00:12:26,131 --> 00:12:28,131
my cabbages are your cabbages.
